*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551337 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551337 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player 